# Ownership for the files whose behaviour the architecture actually rests on.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ASSUMING IT IS ENFORCEMENT. On a solo-maintained repository CODEOWNERS cannot
# gate anything: GitHub does not let you approve your own pull request, so turning on "require
# review from Code Owners" would make every PR permanently unmergeable. What this file does buy is
# visibility — GitHub labels the owned paths in the PR "Files changed" view, so a diff that touches
# load-bearing code announces itself instead of blending into the noise.
#
# The REAL enforcement for these paths is the test suite, and that distinction matters:
#
#   dag.go                  -> dag_test.go (Kahn determinism over 50 randomised runs, cycle detection)
#   valuation.go            -> valuation_test.go (FIFO depletion, moving average, late-arrival restatement)
#   crdb repository         -> repository_integration_test.go (real CockroachDB, single-transaction atomicity)
#   orchestrator service.go -> failtest_killed_pod.sh + failtest_exactly_once.sh
#   consumer DLQ ordering   -> failtest_dlq_poison.sh (wire AND semantic poison pills)
#
# If you are about to change something here, the question is not "am I allowed to touch this file",
# it is "which of those tests proves I did not break it". A change that no test objects to is either
# safe or a gap in the suite — and the second case is the one worth chasing.

